#behavioral questions and answer
Describe a major change that occurred in a job that you held. How did you adapt to this change?,"In my previous role as a software developer our company shifted from a monolithic architecture to microservices. I adapted by quickly learning containerization with Docker and Kubernetes attending training sessions and collaborating with senior engineers. This helped me contribute to the migration and improved my ability to handle technological shifts."
Tell us about a situation in which you had to adjust to changes over which you had no control. How did you handle it?,"During a cloud migration project the vendor changed their API unexpectedly which broke some of our integrations. Although this was beyond my control I reviewed the new API docs updated our scripts and tested thoroughly. This allowed us to meet deadlines despite the disruption."
Tell us about a time that you had to adapt to a difficult situation.,"When a production server crashed during peak traffic I had to adapt quickly. I coordinated with the infrastructure team implemented a temporary failover and worked overnight to restore services. The site was back online with minimal downtime and later we added monitoring tools to prevent recurrence."
What do you do when priorities change quickly? Give one example of when this happened.,"In one sprint we were working on a reporting feature when a critical security vulnerability was discovered. Priorities shifted immediately. I paused my feature work collaborated with the security team patched the vulnerability and verified fixes. This quick response protected user data without delaying other deliverables too much."
Describe a project or idea that was implemented primarily because of your efforts. What was your role? What was the outcome?,"I proposed introducing automated unit testing in our CI/CD pipeline. I created the initial test suite presented the benefits to the team and integrated it with Jenkins. Within a few months test coverage increased significantly reducing production bugs by 30%."
Describe a time when you made a suggestion to improve the work in your organization.,"I noticed code reviews were taking too long due to lack of clear guidelines. I suggested introducing a code review checklist with best practices. The team adopted it and review time dropped while code quality improved."
Give an example of an important goal that you set in the past. Tell about your success in reaching it.,"I set a goal to earn an AWS Solutions Architect certification within six months to better support cloud migration projects. I studied after work completed practice labs and passed the certification exam. This knowledge allowed me to lead the migration of two key services to AWS."
Give two examples of things you've done in previous jobs that demonstrate your willingness to work hard.,"First I once stayed overnight during a critical system outage troubleshooting until services were restored. Second I volunteered to take on additional DevOps responsibilities learning new tools outside of my job description to ensure smooth deployments."
How do you handle a challenge? Tell me about a situation where you faced a challenge.,"During my internship at a tech company I was assigned to implement an API integration I had never worked on before. I researched the documentation sought guidance from peers and tested iteratively. In the end the integration worked successfully and I gained confidence in tackling unknown technologies."
Can you work effectively under pressure? If yes how?,"During a product launch our payment gateway integration failed just hours before release. I quickly diagnosed the issue patched the code and tested it under pressure. The launch went ahead on time and the team recognized my calm approach in high-pressure scenarios."
When was the last time you made a mistake and how did you handle it?,"I once pushed code to production without running all tests which caused a minor bug in the UI. I immediately informed my manager rolled back the deployment and fixed the issue. I also introduced a pre-deployment checklist to prevent similar mistakes in the future."
How do you set goals? Give an example.,"I use the SMART framework to set goals. For example I set a goal to learn Python for automation within three months. I broke it into weekly learning milestones practiced with small scripts and by the end automated reporting tasks for my team."
Take me to the time you set a goal and achieved it.,"I wanted to improve system monitoring in our IT environment. I set a goal to implement Prometheus and Grafana dashboards within a quarter. I learned the tools set up data exporters and created dashboards. The team now relies on them to proactively monitor system health."
Tell me a decision that other team members or colleagues didn't like. How did you implement it?,"As a QA lead I enforced mandatory peer code reviews before merges which some developers resisted due to extra time. I explained how it would reduce bugs demonstrated early results and eventually the team accepted it after seeing improved code quality."
Are you someone who works in a team or like solo working? Give an example of how you worked on a team.,"During a DevOps project I worked closely with developers and sysadmins to build a CI/CD pipeline. Each member handled specific tasks but we collaborated daily. The teamwork paid off—we reduced deployment time from hours to minutes."
What do you do when your opinions collide with someone else?,"While designing a database schema I disagreed with a teammate about normalization levels. Instead of insisting I created two prototypes with performance tests. Presenting data helped us reach consensus and we chose the more efficient design."
Give an example of how you were able to motivate employees or co-workers.,"When our sprint velocity dropped I encouraged the team by suggesting pair programming sessions for tricky tasks. This built collaboration boosted morale and increased overall productivity."
Have you handled a difficult situation? How?,"Once during a cybersecurity incident malware was detected on multiple endpoints. I quickly isolated affected machines coordinated with security and helped restore backups. The incident was resolved with no major data loss."
Describe a project or situation that best illustrates your analytical skills. What did you do?,"During my final year IT project I analyzed system logs to detect anomalies. I wrote scripts to process data and identified unusual spikes linked to memory leaks. The insights led us to fix the root cause and improve performance by 25%."
Describe a time when you had to analyze data and make a recommendation. What kind of thought process did you engage in? Was the suggestion accepted? If not, why not?,"While working as a data intern I analyzed user activity logs to recommend which features to prioritize. I noticed heavy usage of reporting features and suggested improving them. Management accepted the recommendation which increased user retention."
Describe a time when you had to solve a difficult problem. What exactly did you do? What was the end result? What would you have done differently?,"Our automated deployment pipeline was failing randomly. I traced logs isolated the issue to inconsistent environment variables and standardized configurations. The failures stopped. If I could do it differently I would have added environment validation earlier."
How do you research a problem before making a decision? Why?,"When choosing between SQL and NoSQL for a project I researched scalability consistency and cost trade-offs. I reviewed documentation benchmarks and case studies before recommending NoSQL. The system scaled effectively afterward."
When was the last time you had to deal with an angry customer? What exactly did you do? How did things turn out?,"A client was upset about a system outage. I calmly explained the issue gave regular status updates and provided a temporary workaround. They appreciated the transparency and trust was maintained."
If you have inherited a customer, tell me about that experience. What steps did you take to establish rapport with them? What did you do to gain their trust?,"When I took over a client account for an IT support contract I introduced myself via a kickoff call reviewed past tickets and proactively resolved open issues. The client appreciated the smooth handover and continued their contract."
How have you managed a previous situation in which your client changed the brief or 'moved the goalposts'?,"In a software project the client changed requirements midway to include mobile support. I worked with my team to adjust timelines redesigned APIs for compatibility and delivered successfully. The client was satisfied despite the changes."
Describe a time when you went above and beyond to ensure a customer received the best possible service from you and the organization. What was their response?,"A client faced a major bug just before a product demo. I stayed late debugged the issue and delivered a patch overnight. The client was grateful and praised our dedication."
When was the last time you went out on a limb to defend a customer? What occurred?,"A client disputed extra cloud charges due to a misconfigured resource. I gathered evidence escalated to the vendor and secured a refund. The client was very satisfied with the advocacy."
Tell me about a recent successful speech or presentation you gave.,"I presented a workshop on Git branching strategies for my team. I included examples live demos and best practices. Feedback was positive and several developers adopted the new workflow."
When was the last time you had to give a presentation to a group of people with little or no preparation? What challenges did you face? How did you deal with them?,"When a teammate fell sick I had to present sprint progress to stakeholders on short notice. I reviewed Jira boards quickly highlighted completed work and fielded questions confidently. The stakeholders were impressed with my preparedness."
Have you ever had to 'sell' a concept to colleagues? How did you manage it?,"I proposed adopting Docker for local development. To persuade colleagues I created a proof-of-concept showing how it eliminated works on my machine issues. They agreed to adopt it which streamlined development."
Tell me about a time when you were able to effectively communicate with another person, even if that person did not like you (or vice versa).,"A senior developer initially dismissed my ideas during code reviews. Instead of reacting defensively I calmly explained my reasoning and provided documentation. Over time we built mutual respect and collaborated well."
Have you ever encountered any hurdles or difficulties in communicating your ideas to a manager?,"Yes I once struggled to convince my manager about upgrading legacy servers. She was worried about downtime. I prepared a cost-benefit analysis with a phased migration plan. She approved it and we upgraded without issues."
Tell me about a time when you had to use your written communication skills to convey an important message.,"As a system admin I drafted a detailed incident report after a major outage explaining root cause resolution steps and preventive measures. The report was well-received by management and used as a training resource."

#Cyber security
What is the difference between symmetric and asymmetric encryption.,"Symmetric encryption uses a single secret key for both encryption and decryption while asymmetric encryption uses a pair of public and private keys. Symmetric is faster but less secure for key distribution whereas asymmetric is slower but provides better key management and security."
What is a firewall and how does it work.,"A firewall is a network security device that monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predefined rules. It acts as a barrier between trusted internal networks and untrusted external networks blocking unauthorized access and permitting legitimate communication."
Explain the concept of Zero Trust Security.,"Zero Trust Security is a framework that assumes no user or system whether inside or outside the organization should be automatically trusted. Every access request is authenticated authorized and continuously validated using least privilege principles and microsegmentation."
What is the CIA triad in cybersecurity.,"The CIA triad represents the three core principles of information security which are Confidentiality ensuring only authorized users can access data Integrity ensuring data is not altered or tampered with and Availability ensuring systems and data are accessible when needed."
What are IDS and IPS in cybersecurity.,"An Intrusion Detection System IDS monitors network or system activities for malicious behavior and alerts administrators while an Intrusion Prevention System IPS not only detects but also takes proactive actions to block or mitigate the threat in real time."
What is phishing and how can it be prevented.,"Phishing is a cyberattack where attackers trick users into revealing sensitive information like passwords or financial data usually via fraudulent emails or websites. Prevention methods include user awareness training email filtering multi factor authentication and anti phishing tools."
Explain the difference between hashing and encryption.,"Hashing is a one way function that converts data into a fixed length string used for integrity verification whereas encryption is a reversible process that converts data into ciphertext to protect confidentiality and requires keys for decryption."
What is penetration testing.,"Penetration testing also known as ethical hacking is a simulated cyberattack performed by security professionals to identify vulnerabilities in systems networks or applications. It helps organizations strengthen defenses by finding and fixing weaknesses before attackers exploit them."
What are common types of malware.,"Common types of malware include viruses that attach to files and spread worms that replicate themselves without user intervention trojans that disguise as legitimate software ransomware that encrypts files and demands payment and spyware that secretly collects information."
Explain the difference between black hat white hat and grey hat hackers.,"Black hat hackers are malicious attackers who exploit vulnerabilities for personal gain or harm white hat hackers are ethical professionals who test systems to improve security and grey hat hackers fall in between sometimes breaking laws but without malicious intent."
What is two factor authentication and why is it important.,"Two factor authentication 2FA adds an extra layer of security by requiring two forms of identification usually something the user knows like a password and something the user has like a mobile code. It reduces risks of unauthorized access even if passwords are stolen."
What is SQL Injection attack.,"SQL Injection is a code injection technique where attackers insert malicious SQL queries into input fields to manipulate databases. It can be prevented by using prepared statements parameterized queries and input validation."
Explain the difference between vulnerability threat and risk.,"A vulnerability is a weakness in a system that could be exploited a threat is any event or actor that could exploit the vulnerability and a risk is the potential damage or loss that could occur if the threat successfully exploits the vulnerability."
What is social engineering in cybersecurity.,"Social engineering is the manipulation of people into revealing confidential information or performing actions that compromise security. Examples include phishing pretexting baiting and tailgating. Training and awareness are key defenses against social engineering attacks."
What are security patches and why are they important.,"Security patches are software updates released by vendors to fix vulnerabilities and bugs. They are crucial because unpatched systems are prime targets for attackers. Regular patch management ensures systems stay protected against known exploits."
What is a DDoS attack and how can it be mitigated.,"A Distributed Denial of Service DDoS attack occurs when multiple compromised systems flood a target server or network with traffic making it unavailable. Mitigation techniques include traffic filtering load balancing rate limiting and using DDoS protection services."
Explain Public Key Infrastructure PKI.,"Public Key Infrastructure PKI is a framework of policies and technologies that manages digital certificates and public key encryption. It ensures secure communication by verifying identities using certificate authorities CA and enabling encryption and digital signatures."
What is ransomware and how can organizations defend against it.,"Ransomware is malware that encrypts files or systems and demands ransom for decryption. Defense strategies include regular data backups security awareness training endpoint protection multi factor authentication and maintaining updated software."
What is the principle of least privilege.,"The principle of least privilege states that users and systems should only have the minimum level of access required to perform their tasks. This reduces the attack surface and limits damage in case of compromised accounts."
What are honeypots in cybersecurity.,"Honeypots are decoy systems or networks set up to attract attackers and study their methods. They help security teams gather intelligence on threats detect intrusions early and improve defensive strategies without risking real systems."

What is cybersecurity.,"Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting computer systems networks applications and data from unauthorized access cyberattacks and damage. It ensures confidentiality integrity and availability of information while safeguarding businesses and individuals from risks such as malware phishing ransomware and data breaches."

Explain the CIA triad.,"The CIA triad stands for Confidentiality Integrity and Availability. Confidentiality ensures that information is accessible only to authorized users. Integrity guarantees that the data is accurate and has not been tampered with. Availability ensures that systems and data are accessible whenever needed by authorized users. Together these three principles form the foundation of cybersecurity practices."

What is the difference between a vulnerability a threat and a risk.,"A vulnerability is a weakness or flaw in a system that can be exploited. A threat is a potential danger that could exploit a vulnerability such as a hacker or malware. A risk is the likelihood that a threat will exploit a vulnerability and cause damage. For example an unpatched server is a vulnerability a hacker is a threat and the possibility of data theft is the risk."

What is a firewall.,"A firewall is a security device or software that monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predetermined rules. It acts as a barrier between trusted internal networks and untrusted external networks such as the internet. Firewalls help prevent unauthorized access and protect systems from attacks by filtering packets and enforcing security policies."

Explain IDS and IPS.,"IDS stands for Intrusion Detection System which monitors network traffic and alerts administrators when suspicious activities are detected. IPS stands for Intrusion Prevention System which not only detects but also prevents or blocks the malicious activity. IDS is passive whereas IPS is active in stopping attacks."

What is encryption.,"Encryption is the process of converting plain text into unreadable ciphertext using algorithms and keys. It ensures that only authorized parties with the correct key can access the original information. Encryption is widely used to protect sensitive data such as passwords communications and financial transactions."

What is the difference between symmetric and asymmetric encryption.,"Symmetric encryption uses the same key for both encryption and decryption making it faster but requiring secure key distribution. Asymmetric encryption uses two keys a public key for encryption and a private key for decryption which solves the key distribution problem but is slower. Both are often combined for secure communications."

What is multi factor authentication.,"Multi factor authentication MFA is a security method that requires users to provide two or more independent credentials to verify their identity. This may include something you know like a password something you have like a phone or token and something you are like a fingerprint. MFA reduces the chances of unauthorized access."

Explain hashing and how it is used in cybersecurity.,"Hashing is the process of converting data into a fixed length string of characters called a hash using algorithms like SHA 256. It is a one way process meaning it cannot be reversed. Hashing is used to verify data integrity store passwords securely and detect changes in files or messages."

What is social engineering.,"Social engineering is a manipulation technique where attackers trick people into revealing sensitive information or performing actions that compromise security. Common examples include phishing pretexting baiting and tailgating. Since it targets human behavior it is often harder to defend against than purely technical attacks."

What is the difference between black hat white hat and grey hat hackers.,"Black hat hackers are malicious attackers who exploit vulnerabilities for personal gain or to cause damage. White hat hackers are ethical security professionals who test systems to find and fix vulnerabilities. Grey hat hackers fall in between sometimes exploiting vulnerabilities without permission but without malicious intent."

What is a DDoS attack.,"A Distributed Denial of Service DDoS attack occurs when multiple compromised systems usually part of a botnet flood a target system or network with traffic overwhelming its resources and making it unavailable to legitimate users. It is a common attack to disrupt online services."

What is malware.,"Malware short for malicious software is any software intentionally designed to cause harm to a computer system network or user. Types of malware include viruses worms Trojans ransomware spyware and adware. Malware can steal data damage systems or disrupt operations."

What is ransomware.,"Ransomware is a type of malware that encrypts a victim's files and demands payment usually in cryptocurrency for the decryption key. It is a major cybersecurity threat affecting businesses and individuals and prevention relies on regular backups strong security measures and user awareness."

Explain zero day vulnerability.,"A zero day vulnerability is a security flaw in software or hardware that is unknown to the vendor and has no patch available. Hackers exploit these vulnerabilities before they are discovered or fixed making them highly dangerous. Zero day exploits are often used in advanced cyberattacks."

What is penetration testing.,"Penetration testing or ethical hacking is a simulated cyberattack performed by security professionals to identify vulnerabilities in systems applications and networks. The goal is to test how well defenses work and provide recommendations to strengthen security before real attackers exploit the weaknesses."

What is the difference between vulnerability assessment and penetration testing.,"A vulnerability assessment identifies and prioritizes security weaknesses in systems through automated scanning and analysis. Penetration testing goes further by actively exploiting vulnerabilities to determine the real world impact. Both are important but serve different purposes in cybersecurity."

What is phishing.,"Phishing is a social engineering attack where attackers impersonate trusted entities through emails messages or websites to trick users into revealing sensitive information like passwords or credit card numbers. Variants include spear phishing whaling and smishing."

What is patch management and why is it important.,"Patch management is the process of updating software applications and systems with fixes for vulnerabilities bugs and performance issues. It is crucial because unpatched systems are common targets for attackers. Regular patching reduces risk and ensures systems remain secure."

What is two factor authentication.,"Two factor authentication 2FA is a subset of multi factor authentication that requires users to provide two types of evidence to verify their identity typically a password and a code sent to a phone or email. It adds an extra layer of security against unauthorized access."

21. What is the difference between IDS and IPS.,"An Intrusion Detection System IDS only monitors network or host activity and generates alerts when it detects suspicious behavior but it does not block the traffic. An Intrusion Prevention System IPS on the other hand not only detects suspicious activity but also takes automated action such as blocking malicious traffic or dropping packets. IDS is passive while IPS is active in prevention."  
22. What are common types of cyber attacks organizations face today.,"Common cyber attacks include phishing social engineering ransomware denial of service attacks man in the middle attacks SQL injection cross site scripting credential stuffing and insider threats. Each attack exploits a different vulnerability in people systems or networks making layered security essential."  
23. Explain the CIA triad in cybersecurity.,"The CIA triad stands for Confidentiality Integrity and Availability. Confidentiality ensures that sensitive information is accessed only by authorized individuals. Integrity ensures that data remains accurate and unaltered unless changed by authorized users. Availability ensures that systems and data are accessible to users when needed. The CIA triad is the foundation of all cybersecurity practices."  
24. What is a zero day vulnerability.,"A zero day vulnerability is a previously unknown flaw in software or hardware that attackers exploit before the vendor releases a patch. Because the flaw is unknown to the vendor defenders have zero days to prepare or patch it. Zero day attacks are highly dangerous and often used in targeted or advanced persistent threats."  
25. How does multi factor authentication improve security.,"Multi factor authentication MFA adds additional layers of security by requiring users to provide two or more independent forms of verification such as a password something you know a security token something you have or a fingerprint something you are. Even if a password is compromised attackers cannot access the account without the second factor."  
26. What is social engineering and how can it be prevented.,"Social engineering is a method attackers use to manipulate people into giving away confidential information or performing actions that compromise security. Examples include phishing pretexting baiting and tailgating. Prevention involves employee awareness training email filtering enforcing strict security policies and verifying identities before sharing sensitive data."  
27. What is the principle of least privilege and why is it important.,"The principle of least privilege means that users processes and systems are granted the minimum level of access necessary to perform their job functions. It reduces the attack surface prevents unauthorized access and limits the potential damage caused by compromised accounts. Implementing least privilege is a core security best practice."  
28. Explain symmetric vs asymmetric encryption.,"Symmetric encryption uses a single key for both encryption and decryption making it fast but requiring secure key distribution. Asymmetric encryption uses a pair of keys a public key for encryption and a private key for decryption. It is more secure for communications but computationally slower. Often both methods are combined in real world systems such as TLS."  
29. What are firewalls and how do they work.,"A firewall is a security device or software that monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predefined security rules. It establishes a barrier between a trusted internal network and untrusted external networks such as the internet. Firewalls can be packet filtering stateful inspection proxy based or next generation with intrusion detection and application control features."  
30. What is the difference between vulnerability threat and risk.,"A vulnerability is a weakness in a system that could be exploited. A threat is a potential event or actor that could exploit the vulnerability. A risk is the potential impact when a threat exploits a vulnerability. For example an unpatched server is a vulnerability ransomware is a threat and the possible data loss or downtime is the risk."  
31. What is a man in the middle attack.,"A man in the middle MITM attack occurs when an attacker secretly intercepts and possibly alters communication between two parties who believe they are directly communicating with each other. Examples include eavesdropping on unsecured WiFi or DNS spoofing. Using HTTPS VPNs and strong encryption can prevent MITM attacks."  
32. What is penetration testing and why is it important.,"Penetration testing is a simulated cyber attack conducted by security professionals to identify exploitable vulnerabilities in systems applications or networks. It helps organizations discover weaknesses before real attackers do. Pen testing improves security posture verifies defenses and ensures compliance with standards like PCI DSS or ISO 27001."  
33. What is the difference between black hat white hat and gray hat hackers.,"Black hat hackers exploit vulnerabilities for malicious purposes such as stealing data or causing damage. White hat hackers are ethical hackers who use their skills to find and report vulnerabilities legally to improve security. Gray hat hackers fall in between sometimes violating rules but without malicious intent often disclosing flaws without authorization."  
34. What is ransomware and how can organizations defend against it.,"Ransomware is malicious software that encrypts files or locks systems and demands payment usually in cryptocurrency for the decryption key. Defense strategies include regular data backups patch management strong email filtering user awareness training and endpoint protection solutions. Recovery without paying ransom is possible with proper backups and incident response planning."  
35. Explain the difference between blacklisting and whitelisting in cybersecurity.,"Blacklisting blocks known malicious entities such as IP addresses domains or applications. Whitelisting only allows explicitly approved entities and blocks everything else. Whitelisting is more secure but harder to maintain while blacklisting is easier but less comprehensive. Organizations often use a hybrid approach for balance."  
36. What are DDoS attacks and how can they be mitigated.,"A Distributed Denial of Service DDoS attack occurs when multiple compromised systems flood a target server network or application with traffic overwhelming it and making it unavailable. Mitigation includes using content delivery networks load balancing rate limiting intrusion prevention systems and traffic scrubbing services."  
37. What is the role of encryption in protecting data.,"Encryption protects data by converting it into unreadable ciphertext that can only be converted back into plaintext with the correct key. It ensures confidentiality during transmission or storage even if unauthorized parties gain access. Strong encryption algorithms like AES and RSA are critical in securing communications and sensitive data."  
38. What is two factor authentication and how is it different from multi factor authentication.,"Two factor authentication 2FA requires exactly two types of verification factors such as a password and a one time code. Multi factor authentication MFA refers to two or more factors. So 2FA is a subset of MFA. MFA generally provides stronger security as it may include biometric factors and hardware tokens in addition to passwords."  
39. What are honeypots in cybersecurity.,"A honeypot is a decoy system or network resource designed to attract attackers. It simulates vulnerabilities to lure hackers and observe their behavior. Honeypots help security teams gather intelligence on attack techniques detect intrusions early and divert attackers away from real assets. However they must be isolated to avoid being used as pivot points."  
40. What is an advanced persistent threat APT.,"An APT is a prolonged and targeted cyber attack in which an attacker gains unauthorized access to a network and remains undetected for a long time. APTs are often state sponsored or highly organized and their goals include espionage or stealing sensitive data. Defending against APTs requires layered security monitoring anomaly detection and rapid incident response."  
41. Explain what an Intrusion Detection System (IDS) is and how it differs from an Intrusion Prevention System (IPS).,"An Intrusion Detection System (IDS) monitors network traffic or system activities for malicious behavior or policy violations and generates alerts when suspicious activity is detected while an Intrusion Prevention System (IPS) not only detects but also actively blocks or prevents the malicious activity in real time The IDS is passive as it only alerts whereas IPS is active and can drop packets reset connections or quarantine systems making IPS more proactive."  
42. What is a firewall and how does it work.,"A firewall is a network security device that monitors and filters incoming and outgoing traffic based on predetermined security rules It acts as a barrier between trusted internal networks and untrusted external networks like the internet Firewalls can be hardware software or cloud based and they work by permitting or blocking data packets based on rules such as IP address port protocol and application level filtering."  
43. What is the difference between symmetric and asymmetric encryption.,"Symmetric encryption uses the same key for both encryption and decryption making it fast but requiring secure key distribution Asymmetric encryption uses a pair of keys a public key for encryption and a private key for decryption which solves the key distribution problem but is computationally slower Often systems use symmetric encryption for data transfer and asymmetric for key exchange."  
44. What is a DDoS attack and how can organizations defend against it.,"A Distributed Denial of Service DDoS attack occurs when multiple compromised systems flood the bandwidth or resources of a targeted system causing service disruption Defenses include implementing firewalls and intrusion prevention systems rate limiting using DDoS protection services like Cloudflare or AWS Shield deploying load balancers and having an incident response plan to mitigate the impact."  
